session:claudeSonnet5 a prov:SoftwareAgent ;
    rdfs:label "Claude Sonnet 5" .

session:pr774 a agp:AgentSession ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith session:claudeSonnet5 ;
    prov:used ghissues:591 ;
    prov:startedAtTime "2026-11-07T21:56:14Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-11-07T23:25:36Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

session:pr774Summary a agp:TranscriptSummary ;
    agp:summaryText "Issue #591 asked whether a Dexpi2Imf manual test's report of deletion not propagating to inferred facts pointed to a real bug, following #590's conclusion that the datalog crate's core apply_deletions algorithm was not the source for two tested scenarios. Rather than re-testing #590's scenarios, audited sparql_endpoint's write paths for places that mutate the store without notifying the active IncrementalReasoner -- the same bug class #590 had already ruled out at the algorithm level, but never checked at the HTTP-handler level. Found two genuine asymmetries by comparing each write path against its correctly-implemented sibling: (1) dataset_routes.rs's per-dataset /{name}/update route's owl:Nothing rollback reverted only the base store delta with raw remove_quad/add_quad, never calling apply_reasoner_delta, unlike the equivalent rollback in query.rs (root /sparql) and transaction_routes.rs (BEGIN/COMMIT) -- the existing owl:Nothing test suite only ever exercised the root route, so this had zero coverage; (2) CLEAR/DROP (default graph, named graph, ALL, NAMED) in sparql_update.rs called store.remove_graph directly, never routing the removed quads through the reasoner at all, unlike graph_store.rs's gsp_delete_inner (the semantically equivalent Graph Store Protocol DELETE), which already did this correctly. The CLEAR/DROP bug is the stronger candidate for the actual report: it only manifests when a derived fact lands in a *different* graph than its supporting base fact (the common RML-mapped-data shape: extensional data in a named graph, derived facts in the default graph), since a bare remove_graph happens to look consistent whenever base and derived facts share a graph (both get physically wiped together) -- the first two same-graph regression tests written passed even on the buggy code, and only a deterministic cross-graph test (confirmed red, then green after the fix) actually proved the bug. Property-path DELETE WHERE (the shape Dexpi2Imf's real queries use, (imf:connectedTo|^imf:connectedTo)*) was investigated and ruled out -- it correctly propagates into dependent inferred facts for the tested transitive-closure rule shape, via both a + path and a bidirectional alternation-star path." ;
    agp:reasoningFor ghpull:774 ;
    agp:decisionPoint session:pr774Decision ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy session:pr774 ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo session:claudeSonnet5 .

session:pr774Decision a agp:Decision ;
    agp:summaryText "Fixed CLEAR/DROP by adding a clear_graphs_through_reasoner helper that mirrors gsp_delete_inner exactly: collect the target graph(s)' quads and route them through one apply_reasoner_delta call (not one call per graph, so a cross-graph rule doesn't see a partially-cleared intermediate state across ClearAll/ClearNamed's multiple graphs), falling back to a bare remove_graph only when no reasoner is configured. Also explicitly bumps store.generation in the reasoner-present branch: apply_deletions/apply_insertions mutate quads via the QuadTable level directly, bypassing Datastore's own generation-incrementing wrapper methods, so a CLEAR that is the only op in a request (net inserts/deletes both empty) would otherwise leave the If-Match ETag precondition check and a transaction's optimistic-concurrency check unable to detect the change -- confirmed via grep that no call in datalog::incremental ever goes through a Datastore-level method, so the bump is genuinely load-bearing, not redundant." ;
    agp:alternative "Considered collecting the cleared graphs' quads into the existing pending_deletes buffer (the same buffer PatternUpdate/DeleteData use) rather than calling apply_reasoner_delta eagerly inline -- rejected because CLEAR/DROP's existing eager-apply semantics interact with the request's end-of-loop net-delta set-arithmetic (a quad both deleted and re-inserted in the same request cancels out): a single request shaped like 'CLEAR DEFAULT ; INSERT DATA { same triple }' must leave that triple inserted, which the eager approach (verified by a dedicated regression test) gets right, but routing through pending_deletes would incorrectly cancel the reinsert against the buffered clear." .
